  "Question": "A small bottle of shampoo can hold \\(35\\) milliliters of shampoo, whereas a large bottle can hold \\(500\\) milliliters of shampoo. Jasmine wants to buy the minimum number of small bottles necessary to completely fill a large bottle. How many bottles must she buy?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(11\\)",
    "B": "\\(12\\)",
    "C": "\\(13\\)",
    "D": "\\(14\\)",
    "E": "\\(15\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "Divide the required amount by the amount in one small bottle:\n\\[\n500=35\\cdot14+10.\n\\]\nThus \\(14\\) bottles provide only \\(490\\) milliliters, leaving a shortage of \\(10\\) milliliters. One more bottle supplies that shortage, since \\(15\\cdot35=525\\) milliliters. Jasmine can stop pouring when the large bottle is full, leaving some shampoo in the last small bottle.\n\nFourteen or fewer bottles are insufficient, and fifteen suffice, so the minimum is \\(\\boxed{15}\\) bottles, choice E.",
